\title{Weighted graph}
\date{2026-10-02T13:36:39Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\taxon{Definition}

\p{A \em{weighted} (directed or undirected) graph is a graph where each edge is assigned a \em{weight}. That is, formally, there is a function #{w : E \to W} which assigns to each edge #{e \in E} a weight #{w(e)} taken from a suitable set of weights #{W}.  The weight of an edge #{(u,v)} is also denoted #{w(u,v)} or #{w_{uv}}.}
\p{The weight or length of a [path](00LS) is just the sum of the weights of its edges.}

